  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nFog/mist from the early morning has effectively mixed out under \nclear, sunny skies with subsidence from an upper-level ridge \nsituated over the western half of the CONUS. Surface high pressure \nto the south/southeast of the forecast area has aided in the \ntransport of warm, moist air in the low levels, sending heat indices \nfor most of the forecast area into the triple digits. Areas that \nremain under dense cloud cover from the morning will not see their \nheat indices rise as dramatically as surrounding areas, but \nconditions are still expected to remain muggy and uncomfortable. \nThis will be the story through the rest of the weekend, with \ncontinued advection of warm/moist air from the south under an \namplified shortwave ridge sending heat indices into the 100-110 degF \nrange during the daytime. Overnight lows will not offer much \nreprieve from the heat, staying above the 70s. \n\nSome uncertainty continues to remain with hazardous heat persisting \nthrough the early week. Embedded shortwaves in the upper-level flow \nwill \"chip away\" at the upper-level ridge, placing us closer to its \nperiphery under northwesterly flow. Under these kinds of setups, we \nusually see more opportunities for precipitation, with higher \nuncertainty in its exact timing/placement. However, with any \nprecipitation and subsequent cloud cover, this would aid in cooling \ntemperatures overall, meaning higher heat indices may not be \nrealized. Such is the case Sunday/Monday, with the potential for \nshowers/storms across the northern half of the forecast area. A good \nportion of CAM guidance indicates that precipitation will remain off \ntowards the north and east of the forecast area during the day \nSunday, with a continued strong signal (70-90%) of heat indices \naround 100-110 degF during the day in probabilistic guidance. \nHowever, by Monday, continued disturbances within the upper-level \nflow increase our potential for seeing either precipitation or cloud \ncover entering the forecast area. Presently, our PoPs remain around \n10-15% across northern Missouri, which reflects the lower confidence \nin seeing this precise solution in the coming days. Heat headlines \nremain in effect through Monday evening.\n\nAfter Monday, extreme heat and humidity will begin to ease up as \nwinds shift out of the north with the passage of an upper level \ntrough and associated surface low towards the northeast. Greater \nforecast deviations unfold at this point in the period, with poorer \nresolution of the synoptic environment. However, the general gist of \nthe future forecast is increasing likelihood of shortwave \ndisturbances bringing PoPs back into the forecast through midweek, \nwith the exact timing and placement remaining uncertain at this \ntime.",
